3456175ee572dce542b389ab556c141","metadata":{"body":"<!--\nCHANGELOG.md is generated from merged pull requests. Do not add an entry unless this PR introduces\na deprecation or breaking change.\n-->\n\nMake explicit Reactor contexts authoritative, including root, and share operation/application boundaries between Spring AI and WebFlux clients. This changes the published alpha Reactor library contract for 3.0, not the core OpenTelemetry Context API.\n\nReplace the stored-root workaround for transparent signals with `withoutSubscriptionContextCapture`:\n\n```java\n// Explicitly clear active context during signals:\nContextPropagationOperator.storeOpenTelemetryContext(reactorContext, Context.root());\n\n// Preserve signal-time context without subscription-time capture:\nContextPropagationOperator.withoutSubscriptionContextCapture(reactorContext);\n```\n\n- **Context contract:** Preserve implicit-root transparency, spanless contexts, invalid spans, and retry behavior. Explicitly storing a context replaces the no-capture choice.\n- **Shared boundary:** Add Mono/Flux `runWithContext(source, operationContext, applicationContext)` overloads. Scope upstream subscription, demand, and cancellation to the operation; calls to the immediate downstream subscriber to the application, including root. Downstream tracing subscribers retain their own context policy; the boundary does not carry an inner application context with values across merges. Asynchronous upstream propagation still requires registered hooks.\n- **Client integration:** Reuse the boundary in Spring AI and WebFlux without changing span lifecycle ownership. Defer WebFlux exchange construction into the client context.\n- **Agent compatibility:** Bridge capture control and both boundary contexts through agent-owned state. Preserve Reactor 3.1/Java 8 compatibility, existing two-argument helpers, and annotation lifecycle semantics.","title":"Make Reactor contexts authoritative and share client context boundaries"},"mode":